    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190837": {
      "title": "Executive Director of Clinical Analytics, Research and Outcomes",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Dell Medical School Executive Director of Clinical Analytics, Research and Outcomes Apply now Description The Director of Clinical Analytics, Research, and Outcomes will lead a multidisciplinary team that includes clinical research coordinators and quality and outcomes specialists. The Executive Director will oversee these efforts at Dell Children’s Medical Center and collaborate across a comprehensive healthcare platform that includes a broad range of services. The Executive Director will partner with clinical faculty to accomplish academic and care quality initiatives. These initiatives will expand research initiatives through collaborative engagements across hospital sections as well as other aligned stakeholders within the University of Texas at Austin and Ascension Texas hospitals. The primary goal of this position is to build sustainable, rigorous outcomes and research programs that advance the science and delivery of care for patients with congenital heart disease and their families. This will include establishing high‑quality clinical research, a robust data infrastructure, and a continuous quality measurement platform. The incumbent will be responsible for developing and maintaining data systems to measure key outcomes, leading prospective and retrospective studies, pursuing extramural funding, and partnering with clinical and quality teams to translate findings into practice. This position is a faculty‑level role at the Assistant Professor level or above, with active participation in the education and training of undergraduate, graduate, and medical students; residents; postdoctoral fellows; and other trainees, as well as participation in the academic activities of the Departments of Cardiovascular and Thoracic Surgery and Pediatrics. Key Responsibilities ·       Set and drive the strategic direction for a comprehensive outcomes and research program, including clinical research and data infrastructure for congenital heart disease care. ·       Recruit and mentor research colleagues in successful grant writing and acquisition and build a high-performing team. ·       Oversee the design and conduct of clinical research studies (prospective and retrospective) and clinical registries and databases, ensuring feasibility and alignment with program priorities. ·       Lead the development and implementation of data collection, data management, and analytics processes, including integration of clinical, administrative, and patient‑reported data sources. ·       Partner with quality and clinical operations teams to design, implement, and evaluate quality improvement initiatives that are informed by data and research findings. ·       Disseminate research and outcomes findings to internal and external audiences through reports, presentations, manuscripts, dashboards, visualizations, and other communication tools. ·       Train faculty, staff, and trainees in research methods, data interpretation, and best practices in outcomes and quality research. ·       Foster and create collaborations with Dell Medical School departments, UT Austin partners, and Ascension Texas to advise complex and strategic research and quality initiatives. ·       Oversee, prioritize, and activate research initiatives that lead to high-impact peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and extramural grant funding. Work with the Sponsored Programs Office on behalf of the researchers. ·       Ensure the quality of research work and data, drive research excellence, and maintain research team compliance with relevant regulations and best practices, including human subjects protections, data governance, and institutional policies. Qualifications Required Qualifications ·       Doctoral degree (MD, PhD, or foreign equivalent). ·       Demonstrated leadership or management experience. ·       Experience building and leading clinical research teams and operations, including oversight of data and/or quality improvement functions. ·       Strong knowledge of qualitative and quantitative research methodologies and their application. ·       Strong knowledge of biostatistics and research study design, data architecture, AI, and statistical programs (e.g., R, Python, STATA, SAS, etc.) ·       Strong knowledge of grant writing principles and best practices ·       Qualifications for faculty appointment at The University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School at the level of Assistant Professor or above. Preferred Qualifications ·       MPH (Epidemiology, Biostatistics focus) or equivalent experience in epidemiological or outcomes research. ·       MBA, MHA, or equivalent experience in health care administration or management. ·       5+ years of formal leadership or management experience. ·       Clinical background and experience ·       Experience representing outcomes and clinical research with executive leadership and external partners. ·       Experience working on multiple fast‑paced research projects across the project lifecycle, from study design through dissemination. ·       Excellent communication skills, including the ability to translate complex data and research findings for diverse audiences. Application Instructions Interested and qualified candidates should complete the required application and submit a current CV. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Dell Medical School Department Department of Cardiovascular Surgery Posted Aug 31, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191310": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Positions in Statistics and Data Science (2026-2027)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenure-Track/Tenured Faculty Positions in Statistics and Data Science (2026-2027) Apply now Description The Department of Statistics and Data Science (SDS) at The University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenured or tenure-track faculty positions, at any rank, to begin in August 2027. We seek exceptional scholars whose work advances our ability to learn from data, whether by directly addressing important empirical questions in a chosen area of application, or by deepening the foundations of statistical inference and learning. We are particularly interested in four broad directions. First, we seek candidates in applied statistics, scientific machine learning, or AI for science: methodologists embedded deeply enough in another field that they help shape its scientific questions and build new data-analysis methods in response. The field may be any area of the natural, biomedical, computational, engineering, or social sciences. For such candidates, we regard publication in the leading venues of their chosen field as central evidence of impact, on equal footing with publication in statistics and machine-learning venues. No matter the area, the candidate's record should exhibit methodological advances that statisticians and machine-learning researchers would recognize as such, even if they first appeared in a domain journal. Second, we welcome research in causal inference, experimental design, and related areas concerned with learning from interventions and designing informative studies. This includes foundational and applied work on how interventions are identified and evaluated, how evidence generalizes across settings, and how experiments and other data-collection strategies can be designed to answer important questions. Third, we seek scholars working on statistical theory, including the foundations of machine learning and AI. We are interested in fundamental questions about inference, uncertainty, information, learning, robustness, computation, and decision-making. We value theoretical and computational work that provides new understanding of (or new broad capabilities for) statistics, machine learning, and AI, whether or not it is tied to an immediate application. Fourth, we welcome work that creates broadly useful new computational tools for data-analytic practice, such as statistical computing environments and languages, probabilistic programming systems, scalable inference software, and interactive or AI-assisted tools for data analysis. For such candidates, widespread adoption of their tools by researchers and practitioners is evidence of impact on equal footing with publication. SDS is one of three founding departments of UT's new School of Computing, alongside Computer Science and Information. We welcome candidates whose work creates opportunities for collaboration with colleagues in those departments or elsewhere at The University of Texas at Austin, including through joint appointments. Our department is internationally recognized for its research in statistical methodology and theory, machine learning, applied statistics, Bayesian inference, and biostatistics, and its faculty are committed to excellent teaching in statistics and data science for students from across the School, the University, and in SDS's own degree programs. The department currently has 26 tenured/tenure-track faculty, including 9 joint faculty with primary appointments in other departments. UT Austin is one of the most intellectually vibrant universities in the country, with abundant opportunities for interdisciplinary research within the College of Natural Sciences and School of Computing, and across the Dell Medical School, the Oden Institute for Computational Engineering and Sciences, the Population Research Center, the Machine Learning Laboratory, the Center for Generative AI, and many other research centers across the campus. A partnership with the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC) provides access to world-class computing resources.  The department and university are committed to supporting the professional development of all members of the faculty.  The teaching load for tenured/tenure-track faculty in SDS is two courses per year. Austin, the capital of Texas, is a center for high-technology industry, including companies such as 3M, Amazon, AMD, Apple, Applied Materials, AT&T, Dell, Google, IBM, National Instruments, and Samsung. Much of Austin’s lifestyle is driven by outdoor activities, media, and music. More information about the department is here . Qualifications Candidates should have a doctoral degree in statistics, biostatistics, computer science, machine learning, applied mathematics, or a closely related discipline by August 2027. Candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position are evaluated based on their potential for developing an impactful research program and for becoming excellent in teaching, mentoring, and service. Candidates for a tenured Associate or Full Professor position are expected to exhibit a strong independent program of externally funded research along with established records of excellence in teaching, mentoring, and service. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a cover letter, a CV, and separate statements summarizing the applicant’s contributions and plans in the areas of research, teaching, and mentoring.  As part of the application submission process, candidates will be asked to provide the names and email addresses of three individuals who are willing to supply letters of support via Interfolio. For applicants currently in graduate school, at least two letters should be from faculty members at the candidate’s graduate institution. Candidates applying for a tenured position may decline to provide letters of support at the initial application stage.  A link to a professional website where published papers, preprints, and software packages are available may be provided on the candidate’s CV. Review of a pplications will begin on November 23, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. The department is committed to respecting the confidentiality of the search process. A background check will be conducted on applicants selected for the positions. Questions about the search process should be directed to statjobs@austin.utexas.edu , for the attention of Professors Roger Peng and Alessandro Rinaldo, SDS Faculty Search Committee Co-Chairs. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Statistics and Data Science Posted Sep 14, 2026 Apply by Nov 23, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191765": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Government - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Government at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/non-tenure track positions to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments are made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes there is departmental need for an annual appointment. Qualifications A Ph.D. or extensive professional/occupational experience relevant to political science is required. Previous teaching experience is preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants are invited to submit a detailed curriculum vitae, three letters of recommendation, transcript, and teaching materials. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Government Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191518": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Assistant Professor - Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation Apply now Description The Department of Kinesiology and Health Education at The University of Texas at Austin invites qualified applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation. We are seeking a highly productive scholar with expertise in biomechanical and neuromuscular mechanisms of human movement and their role in preventing the onset, progression, or recurrence of adverse health and behavioral outcomes. The Biomechanics and Neurorehabilitation area of our department combines the traditional fields of biomechanics; motor development, motor control, and learning; and cognitive and neuromuscular function. This program examines multidisciplinary aspects of how people move in a variety of settings, including clinical applications, elite performance, and typical changes related to development and aging. The ultimate goal of our work in this area is to generate research which can translate into the improvement of clinical and functional outcomes for people across different stages of life. Our ideal candidate will have a prevention-oriented research agenda that uses biomechanics and/or neuromuscular control to examine mechanisms related to adverse health and behavioral outcomes or develops interventions targeting these mechanisms and pathways to improve health and behavioral outcomes. Integral to program areas in Kinesiology and Health Education and throughout the college, the candidate will build interdisciplinary collaborations linking biomechanics and neuromuscular control with behavioral, clinical, community, or public health prevention frameworks. We expect the successful candidate to have a strong publication record and to demonstrate a record of, or potential for, securing external grants to support their research. In addition, the successful candidate will show potential to be an excellent mentor and will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching coursework in their areas of expertise and within biomechanics, movement science, and other biomechanics and neurorehabilitation sub-disciplinary areas. We seek an individual who is committed to contributing as a faculty member, researcher, and through service to our academic community. Our department is currently ranked #6 by the National Academy of Kinesiology, and we are located within the College of Education, recently ranked #6 by U.S. News & World Report . Our department is currently home to more than 2,000 undergraduates and almost 300 master’s and doctoral students. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students’ diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in biomechanics, kinesiology, physiology, exercise physiology, or related scientific discipline earned by July 31, 2027 Record of, or potential for, research excellence as demonstrated by refereed publications, extramural funding, and evidence of professional service Experience or capacity to teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students Application Instructions Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, please provide all requested materials by October 15, 2026. Applicants should upload the following items to the Interfolio system: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success. Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information. Letters are not required at the time of application. For additional information about the department, visit our website https://education.utexas.edu/departments/kinesiology-health-education/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Isabella Miluzzo at isabella.miluzzo@austin.utexas.edu . Questions about the position can be directed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Hao Hsiao, at HHsiao@austin.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Kinesiology and Health Education Posted Sep 10, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191506": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Liberal Arts Philosophy - 2026-27 Professional Track Faculty Pool Apply now Description The Department of Philosophy seeks to develop a pool of candidates for possible temporary/nontenured positions to teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses. Positions are not available every semester and are contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Appointments are usually made on a semester-by-semester basis, though sometimes there is departmental need for an annual appointment. Positions are renewable annually, contingent upon budgetary funding and performance. Qualifications A PhD in Philosophy and a record of teaching excellence are preferred. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit a current curriculum vita, recent syllabi, course evaluations, and 3 professional letters of reference. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Liberal Arts Department Department of Philosophy Posted Sep 9, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/190881": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Human Development",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Education Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Human Development Apply now Description The Department of Educational Psychology in the College of Education at the University of Texas-Austin is seeking a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the area of Human Development. The person hired for this position will conduct research examining how developmental processes shape learning and well-being across the lifespan, with particular attention to the cultural, social, and institutional contexts in which development occurs. We are especially interested in scholarship that advances understanding of how cultural values, practices, identities, families, communities, and institutions shape developmental processes. We envision a scholar whose work uses developmental science to address questions of central importance to education broadly defined. Relevant areas include but are not limited to how cognitive, social, emotional, or neurobiological development shapes educational experiences; how developmental processes vary across cultural and sociocultural contexts; how family, school, community, and broader institutional environments interact with individual development; how developmental transitions shape educational experiences and well-being ; or how educational environments can better support development across groups. We particularly welcome scholars who incorporate a developmental neuroscience perspective, while also encouraging applicants who use other rigorous and innovative theoretical, methodological, or interdisciplinary approaches. Across approaches, we seek a scholar whose research can contribute to developmental and educational research, practice, programs, or policy by deepening understanding of how people develop within the contexts in which education occurs. UT-Austin's Department of Educational Psychology has robust graduate programs and is currently ranked #6 among the Best Educational Psychology Programs rated by U.S. News & World Report . The department advances knowledge about the human experience at the intersection of education and psychology through research, teaching, and practice. It provides an interdisciplinary and contemporary approach to learning and development and seeks to enhance the quality of the human experience through rigorous educational and psychological research, and effective translation of findings to policy and practice. The successful candidate will have opportunities to build collaborations across the College of Education and the broader university. Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in Human Development, Developmental Psychology, Educational Psychology, Developmental Science, or a closely related field earned by August 2027. Potential for scholarly leadership (i.e., refereed publications and extramural funding) in the area of human development, with research that examines development within cultural, contextual, and institutional settings and contributes to understanding learning, well-being, and variation in developmental experiences. High potential for securing extramural research funding to support innovative research on human development across diverse populations and settings. Evidence of or potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students from a wide range of backgrounds in areas such as human development, developmental science, developmental research methods, and related topics. Established in 1891, the College of Education at UT-Austin consistently ranks among the nation’s top public colleges of education. It is home to approximately 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students and more than 300 faculty. In addition to its longstanding commitment to academic excellence, the college has an extraordinary record of advancing innovation, opportunity, health and well-being, and educational improvement through research, teaching, and public engagement. With annual research expenditures totaling ~$60 million, the college generates knowledge with direct relevance for schools, communities, health systems, and government agencies across Texas and beyond. We also contribute to the state through the preparation of educators and health professionals and through a large network of external partnerships. Over the past five years, the college has expanded its academic programs and enrollment. The college serves students from major metropolitan areas, smaller cities, rural regions, and border communities in Texas, while also welcoming students from other states and countries. Our students diverse experiences and perspectives strengthen the college’s mission to prepare educators, professionals, researchers, and leaders who can address pressing educational and community needs. The University of Texas at Austin is the state’s leading research university, with approximately $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures and a total enrollment of more than 55,000 students. As a member of the Association of American Universities, UT-Austin provides a robust ecosystem for interdisciplinary research through partnerships with Dell Medical School, the School of Social Work, and many other academic and research units on campus. The campus is situated in Austin, one of the nation’s most dynamic and fastest-growing cities, and it is in a state that offers outstanding opportunities for scholarly inquiry. Texas is home to more than 10% of the nation’s PK–12 student population, and approximately 30% of Texas students speak a home language other than English, collectively representing more than 100 languages. Texas has also experienced significant growth in its healthcare, sports, and education sectors. Together, the scale and complexity of Texas, the dynamism of Austin, and the research strength of UT-Austin offer an exceptional environment for scholars seeking to advance knowledge and improve lives. Application Instructions Review of applications will begin 10/19/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, please provide all requested materials by 10/16/2026. Applicants should upload the following items to the Interfolio system: Cover Letter with contact information Full C.V. Research Statement that includes research accomplishments and future research direction Teaching and Mentoring Statement that describes specific methods used to ensure effective student mentoring and a classroom environment that is welcoming, engaging, and supportive of student learning and success. Up to three representative publications A list of three references with contact information For additional information about the department, see https://education.utexas.edu/departments/educational-psychology/ For questions regarding the application process, please contact Becky Cook ( Becky.Cook@austin.utexas.edu ). Questions about the positions can be directed to the chair of the search committee: Laura Quiñones-Camacho ( laura.quinonescamacho@austin.utexas.edu ) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Education Department Department of Educational Psychology Posted Sep 9, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191411": {
      "title": "Tenured Professor (Nurse Scientist, Any Specialty)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Tenured Professor (Nurse Scientist, Any Specialty) Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin School of Nursing invites applications for senior faculty positions. We seek nationally recognized nurse scientists with established, externally funded research programs and strong records of scholarship, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Successful candidates will be appointed as tenured Professors, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Exceptional candidates currently at the associate professor level who demonstrate a record consistent with advancement to professor at The University of Texas at Austin are also encouraged to apply. The University of Texas at Austin offers an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to engage with scholars, clinicians, and research teams across the University, including Dell Medical School, and its numerous research centers and institutes. The University supports more than 3,500 externally sponsored projects, 150 dedicated research units and centers, and annual research funding exceeding $500 million. The School of Nursing offers BSN, MSN, PhD, and DNP degrees in nursing. Additionally, the School has an alternate entry MSN for persons with baccalaureate degrees outside of nursing, and an accelerated BSN to PhD and BSN to DNP options. The School is committed to advancing nursing science through innovative research, education, clinical practice, and service. The University of Texas at Austin is located in the capitol of Texas. Austin is widely recognized as an innovative and dynamic city offering a high quality of life, abundant cultural and recreational opportunities, and thriving academic, healthcare, and technology sectors. The city has been consistently rated a national creative center that draws talented people from across the world. As an added benefit, the children of professional nurse faculty who are under the age of 25 years are eligible for a full tuition waiver at The University of Texas at Austin. Qualifications Successful Candidates are Expected To: Maintain and advance a nationally recognized, externally funded research program in their areas of expertise. Pursue and sustain competitive extramural research funding from federal agencies, foundations, and other sponsors. Produce high-impact scholarship and contribute to the advancement and translation of nursing science. Develop interdisciplinary and cross-sector collaborations within the School of Nursing, across The University of Texas at Austin, and with the broader healthcare and research community. Mentor junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, doctoral students, and other emerging nurse scientists. Teach and advise graduate and/or undergraduate students consistent with the needs of the School and the candidate’s expertise. Provide leadership and service to the School of Nursing, the University, the community, and the profession. Required Qualifications: Earned research doctorate in nursing. An established record of scholarship appropriate for appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor at The University of Texas at Austin A strong record of peer-reviewed, data-based publications relevant to the candidate's primary area of expertise. A demonstrated record of securing and successfully managing extramural research funding. Evidence of successful mentoring of students, junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, and/or other researchers. Preferred Qualifications: An active program of federally funded research. A sustained record of major extramural research funding appropriate to the candidate's area of expertise. Experience building collaborative research teams and mentoring emerging investigators. Research that complements and expands existing strengths within the School of Nursing and University. Eligibility for RN licensure in the State of Texas Application Instructions Applicants should submit a Letter of Interest (LOI) and Curriculum Vitae (CV) via Interfolio. Materials should be addressed to: Kavita Radhakrishnan, PhD, MSEE, RN, FAAN Dean Laura Lee Blanton Chair in Nursing School of Nursing The University of Texas at Austin Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191398": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, tenure-track",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Assistant Professor, tenure-track Apply now Description The Department of Molecular Biosciences at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level in the field of immunology. We welcome applications from outstanding scientists whose areas of interest span the broader field of immunology, including, but not limited to, foundational mechanisms of immune responses, host-pathogen interactions, neuroimmunology, immune interactions with the microbiome, autoimmunity and inflammation, cancer immunology, hematopoiesis and immune system development, structural immunology, and computational/systems immunology. The Department of Molecular Biosciences is composed of a broad faculty whose research interests span many disciplines including biochemistry, molecular biology, structural biology and biophysics, cell and developmental biology, plant biology, microbiology, immunology and infectious diseases, biotechnology, and systems and synthetic biology. We are excited to welcome collaborative scientists who actively interact with our faculty members and take advantage of our state-of-the-art facilities. Successful candidates are expected to establish and sustain a vibrant research program; serve as dedicated teachers and mentors at both the undergraduate and graduate levels; and assist in the administrative and governance functions of the department, the university, and the profession via service on committees as appropriate.  Applicants at the assistant professor level must have plans to develop an exciting independent research program and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate education. “What starts here changes the world.” UT Austin offers numerous resources and an exciting and interactive environment to foster creative interdisciplinary research. In addition to their research activities, our faculty are committed to the development of state-of-the art courses and real-world training experiences for undergraduate and graduate students as part of our core educational mission. Successful candidates will be eligible for membership in the Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs , which includes the Microbiology Graduate Program , and collectively recruit a talented pool of graduate students each year. We also have outstanding core facilities, including those within the Center for Biomedical Research Support , the Texas Advanced Computing Center , and the Sauer Center for Structural Biology . The department has strong ties to other research units at UT Austin, including the School of Engineering, School of Pharmacy, Department of Computer Science, Oden Institute for Computational Engineering & Sciences , and Dell Medical School. This position offers excellent start-up funds, salary, and laboratory space in a dynamic and highly interactive research environment in the vibrant city of Austin.  Applicants will also be encouraged to apply for support from the Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas ( CPRIT ). Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in a relevant discipline and have an outstanding record of scientific ability as demonstrated by a strong publication record. Application Instructions Applicants should apply through Interfolio with the following documents: Cover letter that includes research area, qualifications, and contact information Curriculum vitae A statement of research interests (3-pages maximum) Three confidential letters of recommendation are required and will be requested through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on October 19, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Molecular Biosciences Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191386": {
      "title": "Tenured Professor (Oncology Nursing)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Nursing Tenured Professor (Oncology Nursing) Apply now Description The University of Texas at Austin School of Nursing invites applications for a senior faculty position in oncology nursing science. We seek an accomplished, nationally recognized nurse scientist with an established program of externally funded research and a strong record of scholarship, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. The successful candidate will be appointed at the rank of tenured Professor, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Exceptional candidates currently at the associate professor level who demonstrate a record consistent with advancement to professor at The University of Texas at Austin are also encouraged to apply. The University of Texas at Austin offers an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and collaboration. Faculty have opportunities to engage with scholars, clinicians, and research teams across the University and its numerous research centers and institutes. The University supports more than 3,500 externally sponsored projects, 150 dedicated research units and centers, and annual research funding exceeding $500 million. The School of Nursing offers BSN, MSN, PhD, and DNP degrees in nursing. Additionally, the School has an alternate entry MSN for persons with baccalaureate degrees outside of nursing, and an accelerated BSN to PhD option and BSN to DNP options. The School is committed to advancing nursing science through innovative research, education, clinical practice, and service. The University of Texas at Austin is located in the capitol of Texas. Austin is widely recognized as an innovative and dynamic city offering a high quality of life, abundant cultural and recreational opportunities, and thriving academic, healthcare, and technology sectors. The city has been consistently rated a national creative center that draws talented people from across the world. As an added benefit, the children of professional nurse faculty who are under the age of 25 years are eligible for a full tuition waiver at The University of Texas at Austin. Qualifications Successful Candidates are Expected To: Maintain and advance a nationally recognized, externally funded program of research focused on oncology, cancer prevention, cancer care, cancer survivorship, symptom science, health outcomes, or other areas relevant to oncology nursing and cancer science. Pursue and sustain competitive extramural research funding from federal agencies, foundations, and other sponsors. Produce high-impact scholarship and contribute to the advancement of oncology nursing science. Develop interdisciplinary collaborations within the School of Nursing and across The University of Texas at Austin and the broader healthcare and research community. Mentor junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, doctoral students, and other emerging nurse scientists. Teach and advise graduate and/or undergraduate students consistent with the needs of the School and the candidate’s expertise. Provide leadership and service to the School of Nursing, the University, the community, and the profession. Required Qualifications: Earned research doctorate in nursing or closely related field. An established record of scholarship appropriate for appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor at The University of Texas at Austin A strong record of peer-reviewed, data-based publications A demonstrated record of securing and successfully managing extramural research funding. Evidence of successful mentoring of students, junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, and/or other researchers. Preferred Qualifications: An active program of federally funded research. A sustained record of NIH or other major extramural research funding. Record of leadership in interdisciplinary cancer or oncology research initiatives. Experience building collaborative research teams and mentoring emerging investigators. Research that complements and expands existing strengths within the School of Nursing and University Eligibility for RN licensure in the State of Texas Application Instructions Applicants should upload a Letter of Interest (LOI) and Curriculum Vitae (CV) via Interfolio. Materials should be addressed to: Kavita Radhakrishnan, PhD, MSEE, RN, FAAN Dean Laura Lee Blanton Chair in Nursing School of Nursing The University of Texas at Austin Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Nursing Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Aug 15, 2027 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191277": {
      "title": "Tenured or Tenure-track Faculty Position in Astronomical Instrumentation",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Tenured or Tenure-track Faculty Position in Astronomical Instrumentation Apply now Description The Department of Astronomy at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for an open rank (assistant, associate, or full professor) faculty position in Astronomical Instrumentation. We welcome applications from candidates with an emphasis on Instrumentation in any field of astronomy, however we will favor applicants planning to contribute to our resources at McDonald Observatory (including the GMT). The search will begin with a target start as early as August 2027 but will continue until the position is filled. Information on departmental and McDonald Observatory resources and research programs can be found at: https://astronomy.utexas.edu . UT Austin is a major founding partner in the consortium for the next-generation 25-m Giant Magellan Telescope (GMT: http://www.gmto.org). Major new facilities are also being developed at the West Texas McDonald Observatory site. UT Austin faculty members have access to the 10-m Hobby-Eberly Telescope equipped with instruments such as the VIRUS wide-field integral field spectrograph, the Low Resolution Spectrograph (LRS2), and the Habitable-zone Planet Finder (HPF) precision-RV spectrograph. McDonald also hosts the HJST 2.7-m telescope with high and low resolution spectroscopic capability spanning the optical (including integral field units) to near infrared; the 2.1-m and 0.8-m telescopes; and a node for the Las Cumbres Observatory (LCO) network. The UT Astronomy Program provides vibrant collaborative opportunities on large astronomical surveys taking place at McDonald Observatory, and many staff play leading roles in surveys using JWST, Roman, HST, TESS, and other national/international facilities. For instrument/technology development, MDO offers easy access on the Austin campus to machine shops, Class-100 clean room spaces, electronics benches, mechanical assembly areas, and optical/photonic test facilities including fiber IFU labs. The in-house large machine shops are equipped with multiple CNC and manual machines and a wire-EDM machine for complex part fabrication, aided by several precision metrology machines. 3D printing machines are available to aid rapid prototyping. MDO's highly experienced Austin-based instrument scientists/engineers are available for collaboration and support for instrument projects. Campus-wide, UT Austin hosts micro-photonics/electronics fabrication/metrology facilities at the Texas Material Institute (https://tmi.utexas.edu/facilities) and the Micro Electronics Research Center (https://mrc.utexas.edu/facilities). Computational resources include the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC: https://www.tacc.utexas.edu), which designs and operates some of the world’s most powerful computing resources, and is known for its leadership in high performance computing, visualization, big data analytics, and machine learning. UT Austin hosts a broad range of Centers and interdisciplinary initiative, including the Center for Planetary Systems Habitability (CPSH), Cosmic Frontier Center (CFC), Wootton Center for Astrophysical Plasma Physics (WCAPP), Weinberg Institute of Theoretical Physics, and the NSF-Simons AI Institute for Cosmic Origins (CosmicAI). The Department of Astronomy is a highly collaborative and collegial community, with a strong culture of mentorship, scientific exchange, and support for early-career faculty. Our faculty, researchers, and students interact across subfield boundaries, and new colleagues are encouraged to develop ambitious new research directions and collaborations across the University. Beyond campus, Austin offers an exceptional quality of life for individuals and families alike, combining the cultural and culinary opportunities of a major city with abundant green space and easy access to the outdoors. Ranked among the top U.S. cities for raising a family, Austin is known for its extensive parks and trail systems, neighborhood pools and swimming holes, family-friendly restaurants and outdoor gathering spaces, and year-round cultural and recreational activities. UT Austin also offers significant resources for faculty with families, including three university Child Development Center locations. Qualifications Applicants should have a Ph.D. in Astronomy, Physics, Planetary Science, or related fields, or an equivalent engineering degree with demonstrated interest in astronomical research. The primary selection criteria will be excellence and leadership in astronomical research with instrumentation as a primary component, as well as a commitment to excellence in teaching and supervision of undergraduate and graduate students. Potential applicant profiles include candidates who will lead development, construction, and/or transformational science with next-generation instrumentation at McDonald Observatory, GMT, and other national/international ground and space-based observational facilities, and candidates who develop technologies for next-generation astronomical facilities. Applicants are encouraged to highlight throughout their application their relevant experiences and skillsets in supporting academic success of all students (e.g., their specific approaches for teaching, mentoring, research and/or scholarly work). Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Submissions should include: a cover letter, a description of research accomplishments and current and future research plans (limited to 5 pages total), a statement of teaching interests, experience, pedagogical methods, and goals (limited to 2 pages), a Curriculum Vitae, and a list of publications. Candidates are also required to arrange for 3 letters of recommendation to be submitted to the interfolio site. Complete applications will be reviewed starting Nov 16, 2026 , and we will consider applications after that date on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The start date could be as early August 2027. For further information about the position, please contact the search committee at astro_inst_search2026@utlists.utexas.edu or the Department Chair, Professor Karl Gebhardt (chair@astro.as.utexas.edu). Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Astronomy Posted Sep 8, 2026 Apply by Nov 16, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191913": {
      "title": "Part-Time Clinical Professor, Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions School of Law Part-Time Clinical Professor, Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic Apply now Description The University of Texas School of Law seeks to hire a part-time clinical professor to teach in its Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic (ECDC). The successful candidate may choose either a half-time or three-quarter-time appointment and will work alongside the clinic’s existing faculty, Professor Frances Leos Martinez, Clinic Director, and Professor Heather Way. The clinical professor will supervise students in their representation of clients and will meet regularly with students to mentor and advise them. The clinical professor will be responsible for ensuring that the clinic’s clients receive competent and professional representation. The professor will also collaborate with the clinic faculty in all aspects of the clinic’s work, including teaching portions of the clinic’s weekly seminar, selecting cases and projects, and participating in activities of the broader clinical program. Clinical faculty positions at Texas Law are nine-month appointments. This position is a part-time, non-tenure-track clinical faculty appointment, with all accompanying health and other benefits. Depending on the needs of the clinic’s operations, cases and projects, an additional part-time summer appointment may be available. The clinical professor will join a close community of over 25 faculty members who teach in Texas Law’s clinical program. The salary range for this half-time position is $75,000 - $86,000 for nine months, depending on experience. The salary for a three-quarter-time appointment will be adjusted proportionally. Any summer appointment would be compensated separately on a pro rata basis. About the Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic The ECDC provides pro bono transactional legal representation to small businesses, social enterprises, creatives, nonprofit organizations, collectives, cooperatives, and community groups in Texas. The clinic’s clients are community enterprises that strengthen their communities by creating jobs, producing and preserving affordable housing, expanding asset-building opportunities for people with low incomes, and providing innovative and valuable goods and services. The ECDC has a dual mission: educating law students and serving low-income communities through the practice of transactional law. More information about the ECDC is available at: https://law.utexas.edu/clinics/ecdc/ Qualifications Membership in good standing with the State Bar of Texas or eligibility for admission to the Texas Bar without examination. At least six years of transactional law experience in one or more relevant practice areas, such as corporate law, nonprofit or tax law, community economic development, microenterprise, intellectual property, or real estate. Experience supervising and mentoring law students or junior attorneys. Familiarity with clinical teaching methods. Preferred Qualifications Prior teaching experience (preferred). Experience working with community groups. Application Instructions To apply, submit a letter of interest, a resume or curriculum vitae, a writing sample, and the name and contact information of three references. Review of applications will begin in October 20, 2026, and will continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The preferred start date for the position is July or August 2027, although the successful candidate may have the option to begin as early as January 2027. Questions about the position should be directed to Professor Frances Leos Martinez, Director of the Entrepreneurship and Community Development Clinic, at fmartinez@law.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit School of Law Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191722": {
      "title": "Faculty Careers",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Fall 2027 - Multiple Positions in Mechanical Engineering - UT Austin Apply now Description The Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering at The University of Texas at Austin seeks applicants for multiple positions. Candidates with expertise in the following research areas are of the most interest to the Department. (Candidates should view these areas of emphasis in the broadest sense in determining whether they match their research skills and interests.) NUCLEAR ENGINEERING, with an emphasis on nuclear materials or radiochemistry. Candidates should be able to demonstrate an ability to teach both nuclear engineering and materials science or mechanical engineering courses at the undergraduate level. ROBOTICS & PHYSICAL AI, with an emphasis on embodied autonomous and semi-autonomous systems that perceive, plan, and act under real-world physical constraints, including in close collaboration with humans, spanning: (a) novel actuator and sensor design, including soft and smart materials and metamaterials, and their integration into robotic systems; (b) physics-inspired machine learning for perception, planning, and control, together with the experimental data collection and simulation tools needed to support it, with a balanced approach to theory and experimental work; (c) applications in assistive and rehabilitation robotics that restore, augment, or support human motor, sensory, or cognitive function, including emerging brain-robot interfaces, and in intelligent automation for complex, unstructured industrial, service, or medical environments. ADVANCED MANUFACTURING , with an emphasis on advancing additive manufacturing as a production technology for aerospace, defense, and other critical applications. Research may span the technologies needed to make additive manufacturing more intelligent, predictable, and certifiable, including machine-learning-enabled process control, in situ sensing and monitoring, data-driven and physics-based modeling, digital twins, materials-by-design, and integrated process-structure-property relationships. Of particular interest are candidates whose work can reduce the time and cost associated with process and part qualification by moving quality assurance from post-build inspection to predictive, in-process understanding and control. The successful candidate will strengthen UT Austin's leadership in production-focused additive manufacturing and advance the field toward reliable, autonomous processes. Candidates at the rank of Assistant Professor are sought for the Nuclear and Robotics searches. In the Advanced Manufacturing search applications are welcomed at both the Assistant Professor and Tenured Associate/Full Professor ranks. Qualifications Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. degree in Engineering or a related field prior to their start date. Successful candidates will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds, with differing learning approaches and abilities, develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in service to the university and the profession. The Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering is committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty. A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to those efforts. More information about the Walker Department of Mechanical Engineering, including a summary of the Department’s Strategic Plan , may be found at www.me.utexas.edu. Application Instructions Application Interested applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio: (1) a cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) a list of references. Please see me.utexas.edu/people/faculty-jobs for additional information and guidance. Successful candidates will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form, a background check, and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the U.S.A. Deadlines For full consideration, applications should be received by November 8, 2026. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Mechanical Engineering Posted Sep 15, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191568": {
      "title": "Two Assistant Professor Positions in Evolutionary Biology and Mechanistic Processes",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Two Assistant Professor Positions in Evolutionary Biology and Mechanistic Processes Apply now Description The Department of Integrative Biology at The University of Texas at Austin seeks to fill two faculty positions in the areas of Evolutionary Biology and Mechanistic Processes, broadly defined. We are looking for candidates who are advancing (1) evolutionary biology or (2) the mechanistic understanding of organismal processes. These positions will be filled at the level of Assistant Professor. Qualifications Candidates should be using innovative approaches in evolutionary biology and/or the mechanistic understanding of whole-organism processes. Mechanistic processes include, but are not limited to, behavior, biomechanics, cognition, physiology, and development. The phylogenetic scope of the Evolutionary Biology position may include eukaryotes, prokaryotes, viruses, and the scale may include individual molecules to entire ecosystems and beyond. Candidates for either position may employ experimental, theoretical, or computational frameworks, or a combination thereof. Special consideration will be given to applicants with demonstrated potential for integrating across our target disciplines. We seek applicants with a strong record of high-quality research, publications, potential for extramural funding, and a commitment to excellence in teaching and mentorship. A PhD or equivalent is required at the time of appointment, and postdoctoral research experience is strongly recommended. Application Instructions Applicants should submit the following items via lnterfolio, indicating the position at https://apply.interfolio.com/193270 : (i) cover letter (1 page), (ii) CV, (iii) research statement (up to 3 pages), (iv) teaching statement (up to 2 pages), (v) mentoring statement (up to 2 pages), (vi) up to 5 reprints/preprints in one PDF document, and (vii) names and contact information for three references. Reference letters will be requested from selected applicants at a later date. Applications received by 15 October will be assured of full consideration. For additional information about the department, see https://integrativebio.utexas.edu/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Integrative Biology Posted Sep 14, 2026 Apply by Oct 15, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191202": {
      "title": "Open Rank Search in Plant Evolution and Director of the UT-Austin Plant Resources Center",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions College of Natural Sciences Open Rank Search in Plant Evolution and  Director of the UT-Austin Plant Resources Center Apply now Description The Department of Integrative Biology at the University of Texas at Austin seeks to fill an open rank faculty position to serve as the Director of the Billie L. Turner Plant Resources Center (PRC). Tenured applicants are preferred, but assistant professors with several years in rank will also be considered. The PRC has a world-class plant collection of 1.1 million specimens in three herbaria ( https://biodiversity.utexas.edu/collections/plants ) and is a part of the Biodiversity Center ( https://biodiversity.utexas.edu ) within the Department of Integrative Biology . The successful candidate will be expected to demonstrate exemplary leadership of the PRC in biodiversity programming. The PRC has three fulltime staff and several large endowments that secure its future funding. E ligible candidates, depending on rank, will be nominated to be the holder or fellow of the Sidney F. and Doris Blake Centennial Professorship in Systematic Botany. Qualifications We prefer candidates with an outstanding record of research that demonstrates innovation in addressing fundamental questions in biodiversity and evolution of plants. Applicants should have a demonstrated successful career, including influential publications, extramural research funding, a commitment to excellent teaching, and experience in herbarium administration and/or collections-based research. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research, teaching and graduate student/postdoctoral training programs actively engage with our plant collections and leverage the opportunities offered by our associated field stations ( https://biodiversity.utexas.edu/field-stations-page ) . Application Instructions Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, one statement summarizing research, teaching, mentoring, experience with collections, pdfs of five reprints/preprints, and names and addresses of three references to Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193263 . Reference letters will be requested from selected applicants at a later date. Applications received by October 15, 2026, will be assured full consideration. For additional information about the department, see https://integrativebio.utexas.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit College of Natural Sciences Department Department of Integrative Biology Posted Sep 14, 2026 Apply by Oct 15, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191781": {
      "title": "Professional Track Faculty Position",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Professional Track Faculty Position - Chemical Engineering - 2026-27 Apply now Description The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering seeks to develop a pool of candidates for professional track faculty positions to teach undergraduate and graduate level courses. Positions are part-time and contingent upon student enrollment and the availability of funding. Positions may not be available every year. The McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering’s undergraduate and graduate programs are both top-10 ranked programs in the nation according to U.S. News & World Report. The department leads pioneering research and education in vital areas such as energy and the environment, human health, materials and manufacturing. Our faculty includes 12 members of the National Academy of Engineering and two National Medal of Technology and Innovation recipients. Our students go on to transform industries, benefit society and improve quality of life. As part of the Cockrell School of Engineering, we’re committed to building a welcoming environment, free from all forms of hate, racism and injustice, where students, faculty and staff feel safe and supported. Every member of our school community — whoever you are, wherever you are from and whatever you believe — is valued. We are proud to be an Equal Opportunity Employer. Qualifications A Ph.D. in chemical engineering or a related field is required, but exceptions can be made for applicants with a Master's degree and pertinent expertise and work experience. Previous teaching and/or training experience is preferred. SALARY: Dependent on position and qualifications. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Interested applicants should submit a detailed resume including academic, training, and technical experience, a statement regarding their specific teaching interests and qualifications, and a list of three references. Further information about the McKetta Department of Chemical Engineering may be found at che.utexas.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Chemical Engineering Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/191743": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Cockrell School of Engineering Assistant Professor, Fall 2027 - Aerospace Engineering Apply now Description The Department of Aerospace Engineering and Engineering Mechanics at The University of Texas at Austin  invites applicants for a full-time tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. The field of aerospace engineering is undergoing revolutionary transformation in 1) Space discovery, observations, and connectivity and in 2) Advanced air mobility solutions.  Applicants with expertise in all disciplines pertinent to these two application areas are encouraged to apply for the position.  We seek exceptional candidates who hold promise for leading these transformations. An emphasis will be placed on applicants who have a clear vision about how they will contribute to these emerging areas of aerospace engineering. The Department of Aerospace Engineering and Engineering Mechanics is committed to supporting the success of all members of our community: students, staff, and faculty. A successful candidate can expect to benefit from and contribute to these efforts. Qualifications Candidates must have a PhD in engineering or a related discipline. The successful candidate will be expected to create undergraduate and graduate learning environments that address the needs of students from a variety of backgrounds, with differing learning styles and abilities. Further, the successful applicant will be expected to develop an externally sponsored research program, mentor graduate students, collaborate with other faculty, and be involved in service to the university and profession. Application Instructions Interested applicants should submit the following materials: (1) a cover letter, (2) current curriculum vitae, (3) research statement, (4) teaching and mentoring statement, and (5) three professional reference letters . Please see https://ae.utexas.edu/people/careers/ for additional information and guidance. Candidates should address their submission to the Aerospace Engineering Faculty Search Committee. Evaluation of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 , and will continue until an appropriate candidate is identified. The successful candidate will be required to complete an Employment Eligibility Verification form and provide documents to verify identity and eligibility to work in the USA. A security sensitive background check will be conducted on the applicant selected. Please address questions to Prof. David Goldstein, the Faculty Search Committee Chair ( david@oden.utexas.edu ) Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Cockrell School of Engineering Department Aerospace Engineering & Engineering Mechanics Posted Sep 11, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192014": {
      "title": "Faculty Position (Tenured)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Chemistry Faculty Position (Tenured) Apply now Description The Department of Chemistry at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of full professor in all areas of chemistry, subject to available funding. We are particularly interested in candidates with exceptional and internationally recognized experimental research programs, who will help to further elevate our research and higher educational profile through their leadership, mentorship, teaching and service. It is anticipated that a successful candidate for this position will be awarded a personal chair to support their future research program at UT Austin. The Department of Chemistry's mission is to provide all our students with an innovative and forward-looking scientific education grounded in the fundamentals of chemistry. The department and University have state-of-the-art core facilities and we are ranked 16th best graduate program in the United States by US News and World Report (2023 ed.). We have a strong commitment to building and maintaining a supportive environment where all people can thrive. Learn more about the Department of Chemistry and key core facilities, including Mass Spectrometry , Nuclear Magnetic Resonance , and the X-Ray Facility . Qualifications PhD in chemistry or closely related field. Application Instructions Complete applications should contain a cover letter indicating area of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a description of research plans, a teaching statement, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation will be requested directly from the letter writers through Interfolio. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Chemistry Posted Sep 16, 2026 Apply by Open until filled Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
    },
    "https://faculty.utexas.edu/career/192011": {
      "title": "Faculty Position (Assistant Professor, Synthetic Chemistry)",
      "description": "Faculty Careers - The University of Texas at Austin UT Austin Home The University of Texas at Austin Faculty Careers All Positions Department of Chemistry Faculty Position (Assistant Professor, Synthetic Chemistry) Apply now Description The Department of Chemistry at the University of Texas at Austin invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in synthetic chemistry, subject to available funding.  Applicants whose future research programs are aligned under the broad themes of synthetic organic, organometallic, inorganic and/or materials chemistry are particularly encouraged to apply.  Successful candidates will contribute to our higher education mission through dynamic teaching, research, and service activities. Applicants are expected to establish and sustain a vibrant research program; serve as dedicated teachers and mentors to our increasingly diverse students at both the undergraduate and graduate levels; and assist in the administrative and governance functions of the department, the university and the profession via service on committees, as appropriate.  Applicants for the position of Assistant Professor must have a promising research agenda, and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate student education and success. The Department of Chemistry's mission is to provide all of our students with an innovative and forward-looking scientific education grounded in the fundamentals of chemistry. The department and University have state-of-the-art core facilities and we are ranked 16th best graduate program in the United States by US News and World Report (2023 ed.).  We have a strong commitment to building and maintaining a supportive environment where all people can thrive. Learn more about the Department of Chemistry and key core facilities, including Mass Spectrometry , Nuclear Magnetic Resonance , and the X-Ray Facility . Qualifications PhD in chemistry or closely related field. Application Instructions Complete applications should contain a cover letter indicating area of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a description of research plans, a teaching statement, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation will be requested directly from the letter writers through Interfolio. The University of Texas at Austin is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Texas at Austin, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. College / School / Unit Department of Chemistry Posted Sep 16, 2026 Apply by Oct 11, 2026 Applying UT Austin uses Interfolio to manage the application process. Need help? Contact help and support . Share Email Facebook LinkedIn Twitter UT Austin Home Learn more about UT Contact us Phone 512-475-4363 Fax 512-475-7385 Email evpp-aps@utexas.edu 110 Inner Campus Dr. STOP G1000 Austin, TX 78712-1701 Careers Search Why UT Life & Work Quicklinks UT Austin Home Site Policies Web Privacy Policy Emergency Information Web Accessibility Policy Adobe Reader"
